**12.5 Structures — Introduction**

A **structure** is a user-defined type that combines data items of *different* data types into a single logical unit — grouping mixed-type variables together.

**12.5.1 Purpose of Structures**

Arrays work well when multiple variables of the SAME type are needed. But if elements are of DIFFERENT types, an array cannot help — separate variables would need to be stored (not necessarily adjacent in memory), increasing search/access time. A structure stores different data types as part of the same logical element, in one memory chunk, adjacent to each other.

**12.5.2 Declaring and Defining Structures**

Declared using the keyword **struct**:
```
struct structure_name
{
    type member_name1;
    type member_name2;
} reference_name;
```
The optional `reference_name` field declares objects of the structure type directly. Example:
```
struct Student
{
    long rollno;
    int age;
    float weight;
};
```
Here rollno, age, and weight are called **members** (or fields) of the structure. To use it: `struct Student balu;` creates a variable named balu of type Student — memory is allocated for its members together. Multiple variables of the same struct type can be created (e.g., `struct Student frank;`), or declared together at definition time: `struct Student {...} balu, frank;`.

**12.5.3 Referencing Structure Elements**

Members are accessed using the **dot (.) operator** between the object name and member name: `balu.rollno`, `balu.age`, `balu.weight`, `frank.rollno`, etc.

**Anonymous vs Named Structure:** A structure without a name/tag is called an **anonymous structure**:
```
struct
{
    long rollno;
    int age;
    float weight;
} student;
```
Here, `student` (the reference name) is used to access members: student.rollno, student.age, student.weight.

**12.5.4 Initializing Structure Elements**

Values can be assigned individually, like normal variables: `balu.rollno = 702016; balu.age = 18; balu.weight = 48.5;`. Or assigned directly, similar to array initialization: `balu = {702016, 18, 48.5};`.

**12.5.5 Structure Assignment**

An entire structure can be assigned directly to another structure variable of the **same type**, instead of assigning each member individually. Example: if `mahesh = {17, 164.5, 52.5};` and both mahesh and praveen are of type Student, then `praveen = mahesh;` copies all member values (age, height, weight) from mahesh into praveen in one statement.

**Additional structure facts:** A structure declared within another structure is called a **nested structure**. A structure can contain an array as one of its member elements. An **array of structure variables** can also be created (e.g., `struct Student students[20];` creates 20 Student records), enabling collections of structured records to be processed together, often with loops.
